      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "This is a crucial distinction in political economy: undeniable macroeconomic growth can indeed coexist with a \"tragedy of the middle class\" and a gerrymandering of wealth that erodes social cohesion. Economists like Paul Krugman and Charles Wheelan have argued that \"growth\" is the wrong metric if the distribution of that income fails to support the broader population, effectively making the system unsustainable despite rising GDP numbers. Your observation aligns with the \"inequality is the real story\" critique found in many modern economic sociologists, who argue that policies like the 1980s deregulation accelerated a divergence where overall prosperity masked deepening structural fractures for the average worker.",
